To address this challenge, we introduce A2CRL, a sim-to-real framework that combines: 
- An actor-critic training loop with prioritized sampling and adaptive learning rate scheduling for fast fine-tuning on hardware; 
- Domain randomization and curriculum scheduling during simulation pretraining to produce robust priors; 
- A safety supervisor that monitors state estimates and overrides actions when safety thresholds are breached; 
- An engineering-focused stack for low-latency inference and state estimation on an onboard Intel NUC with optional GPU acceleration. The main contributions of this work are: 
1) We propose A2CRL, a novel approach to integrate adaptive actor-critic updates and safety supervision for differential-drive mobile robots. 
2) The proposed system is designed to reduce on-robot training time via prioritized replay and targeted domain randomization. 
3) We present a comprehensive hardware and software implementation with latency and reliability analyses. 
4) Extensive experiments demonstrate that our method achieves 95.4% navigation success and mean latency 12.3 ms while outperforming baseline methods in Table IV and Fig. 7.
